			<div class="content" id="post-content-3620820">
									

<blockquote>
<p>PoI-668 will then introduce himself, and attempt to coerce the subject from committing suicide.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Totally read that as &quot;subject <em>into</em> committing,&quot; which made it much more sinister. This was helped by the fact that &quot;coerce&quot; has an implication of force or threats, which made it sound like the hotline bullied people into not killing themselves.</p>
<p>As for the article itself, it feels like it's all over the place. There's cryptocurrency, which is played up as this big thing but turns out to be a red herring. There's a focus on the acronym for no particular reason. There are AI's.</p>
<p>Then for the big reveal:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">This&nbsp;was&nbsp;my&nbsp;takeaway,&nbsp;at&nbsp;least</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>It turns out the dude is an AI, and his sister is the death AI, and he will &quot;do anything if it'll bring her back.&quot;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
How this process relates to what is going on in the article was unclear to me.
<p>Moreover, for all of the dodecatuple-secret-clearance stuff on the last collapsible, it most just felt kinda meh. It's not a &quot;oh shit&quot; moment where disparate parts of the article snap into place. It doesn't force me to reevaluate what I just read. It's mostly just a &quot;huh, okay&quot; moment.</p>
<p>I did like the fact that, although the tropes you used for this (spooky computers, friendly dude chatting) are indicative of how typical spooky articles tend to get written up, the article itself is decidedly not spooky.</p>

